## 15. ESTABLISHED LADAKH THERMAL PHYSICS

- Ladakh is approximately 3000–5000 m above sea level.
- Winter outdoor temperatures regularly reach -15°C to -30°C.
- Daily temperature swings can exceed 25°C–30°C.
- Traditional Ladakhi designs use:
  - South-facing orientation.
  - Thick thermal-mass walls.
  - Minimal openings on non-south facades.
  - Small windows.
- Trombe-wall passive designs have shown approximately 50% fuel-consumption reduction in monitored Ladakh buildings.
- Heat losses occur through:
  - Wall conduction.
  - Roof conduction.
  - Floor conduction.
  - Glazing conduction.
  - Door conduction.
  - Air infiltration.
- A 450 mm rammed-earth wall can have roughly 8–10 hours of thermal lag.
- A 600 mm stone wall can have over 12 hours of thermal lag.
- Peak indoor temperature can trail peak outdoor or solar conditions by many hours.

